 um even though we leave hong kong  and i really do hope that  every bit of actions that i'm taking now  can help and can pave the way for me  for my way back to hong kong asked about  nathan's case  the chinese embassy in london said hong  kong  acts under the rule of law  but the crackdown on pro-democracy  protesters in hong kong  is seen by many china watchers as a sign  of beijing's growing confidence on the  world stage  and it's fueled speculation about the  fate of another problem territory  the democratically governed island of  taiwan  freddie lim is lead vocalist in a  taiwanese heavy metal band  called chasonic he's also  a politician who supports independence  for his island of 23 million people  [Music]  i i think most of the people knows that  uh china send their  warships and uh fighter jets to harass  taiwan uh i think this kind of  harassment  that china down to taiwan has been like  for 20 or 30 years so it's it's kind of  taiwanese kind of uh in one hand we get  used to that  in the other hand we are always be very  cautious about that but something that  i think the world realized or even  taiwanese realized  in recent years is that china also  infiltrate  taiwan with other measures  like they spread this information they  put their hands in our local communities  he made similar allegations of  interference in taiwan by the chinese  government and its supporters  as nathan did about hong kong but while  hong kong is internationally recognised  as being part of china  taiwan's status is much greater beijing  officially claims the territory as its  own  and ensures it remains diplomatically  isolated from other countries  but taiwan's democratically elected  president psying wen  says the island is an independent  country  it's been an unresolved dispute since  1949  unification by force remains a  possibility  though that could draw in the united  states  it's why taiwan is one of the most  sensitive issues  in u.s china relations that is  potentially a very dangerous flash point  and it's a more dangerous flash point  today than it was two or three years ago  alexander downer was australia's foreign  minister  between 1996 and 2007.  going back to before xi jinping's time i  would have said well  um they they still aspire to  reunite the mainland and taiwan and  i had hoped that that would happen  through peaceful means  through a process of negotiation and  through through the evolution of time  the convergence of the political  systems in taiwan and in beijing but  actually  in the last few years you've seen the  exact reverse happen  they have started to diverge and there  are two aspects to that  one is that beijing has taken up  a much more hostile stance towards  taiwan  but secondly it does go to hong kong i  mean  they used to argue in beijing even  including to me  that look what we've done with hong kong  with the one country two systems formula  it's worked well there  life just continues to continue in hong  kong as it always did  um it's not perfectly democratic  according to your standards but then it  wasn't when the british ruled hong kong  which is true so taiwan  can learn from the hong kong experience  of the success of one country two  systems  but of course now taiwan can learn from  the experience of hong kong  which is that beijing introduces a  national security law  and exercises control over security  um over the lives of people in hong kong  um and they would do the same in taiwan  and do you think it could become like a  military conflict  could yeah i mean you could imagine a  scenario where that could happen  i think there's there's a risk of that  they're probably not strong enough  militarily  to do it without paying a terrible price  because it would uh  potentially draw in the united states  and the united states  allies into the conflict so it could  become pretty nasty  for china or everyone but including for  china  it could make the fate of taiwan a  litmus  test for 