 western  perspective  draws an analogy with a moment in the  1997  spy comedy austin powers when arch  villain  dr evil is in group counseling with his  son  scott he says to think of dr evil as the  west  and scott as the chinese communist party  the late carrie fisher of course is the  therapist  and and scott the son is is asked so  what what bothers you and he says  something to the effect of you know i  think  i think my father's trying to kill me  and uh  you know carrie fisher's character you  laughed no no one's trying to you  you know we may say we're they're trying  to kill us but but no one's trying to  kill you  and then dr evil's character sort of  steps in and says no the boy is actually  quite quite right i am trying to kill  him i mean you know  this is what we're trying to do is  undermine  authoritarian regimes i mean it is not  something that we want to see go on now  we don't want  war um we'd love to see them transition  from  you know whether it's a what we saw in  south korea moving away from  an authoritarian sort of military rule  to a fully functioning democracy and  then the the explosion of prosperity  that happened after that or  or the same thing in taiwan in which we  moved away from an authoritarian regime  into something that  that looked very much like i mean a  fully functioning  model democracy uh and which just then  had  enormous prosperity for its citizens  right and so  that that idea is sort of i think at the  fundamental level of what we'd want  to see and so it i think it creates this  dilemma  uh for the prc in which uh to continue  to have  a system that sort of respects  individual rights  threatens their existence and that sets  us up for  rivalry and competition that is likely  to continue  um and and the sooner we kind of kind of  understand those dynamics  probably the better off we will be to  make other decisions  it's a contest that's playing out right  now  in the gray zone regardless of whether  or not  leaders